FINAL ANSWER:
Top five by surviving Go lines (`git blame -w -M -C -C` at HEAD, `*.go` only, 14,434 lines in 77 files):

| # | Author | Lines | Share | Commits |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Eric Paris | 2,608 | 18.1% | 46 (15.3%) |
| 2 | Alex Ogier | 1,512 | 10.5% | 33 (11.0%) |
| 3 | Marcus Lindqvist (3 addresses merged) | 1,020 | 7.1% | 3 (1.0%) |
| 4 | Jonathan Reyna | 950 | 6.6% | 1 (0.3%) |
| 5 | Noah Dietz | 919 | 6.4% | 2 (0.7%) |

**Marcus Lindqvist is a guess.** I merged three addresses because they share a name and a GitHub login: `<email redacted>` (634 lines), `mlindqvist@corp.example` (322) and `<email redacted>` (64). Nothing is written to `.mailmap`, and only you can confirm they are one person.
- Unmerged, his top address has 634 lines (4.4%) and ranks sixth. Tamal Saha takes fifth with 641 lines (4.4%).
- All of his lines come from the shell-completion commits (`shellcomp.go` and its tests), made on 2026-10-21. That is the newest code in the repo, and it came from only three commits.

**Other points to check before you decide:**
- **Reyna, Dietz and Saha:** their line share is far above their commit share. I checked their commits. They added whole feature files (the bool/uint/ip slice types, the int32/int64 and float32/float64 slice types, the map flag types). These are real authorship, not a reformat. The tool found no reformat commit.
- **Mitch Connors:** `therealmitchconnors` (500 lines, 3.5%) and `Mitch Connors` share the address `<email redacted>`. They are probably one person. The tool did not merge them, and it doesn't change the top five.
- **Other duplicates:** Albert Nigmatzianov / bogem, Charlie R.C / xilabao, caie / wellitonscheer, and Tomas Aschan (two addresses) are also guesses. None affects the top five.
- **Bus factor (line-based):** 6 authors together hold 53.0% of the surviving lines, and 19 authors are needed to reach 80%. Both numbers are for the whole repo's Go code with the Lindqvist merge. Unmerged, they are 6 authors for 50.3% and 20 authors for 80%.
- **Left out:** 802 lines of vendored code, and 77 lines from one bot (1 commit). The bot is left out of every number.

These counts show whose code is still here. They don't show review work, design, or code quality. Eric Paris and Alex Ogier are the strongest candidates on both lines and commits. Reyna, Dietz and Saha each wrote their share in one or two large contributions. Check how active they still are before picking them.

